An overlay resolver, Tailscale-shaped but vendor-neutral: a scoped resolver at a
CGNAT address (100.64/10) serving zones nothing else can answer.

The point of the scenario is that such a zone has no working lane. sing-box's
`local` server is Go's resolver, which reads /etc/resolv.conf — on macOS the
PRIMARY resolver alone, never the scoped table — so the zone NXDOMAINs in corp
just as it does in direct. `corp sync` therefore mirrors it into the HOTSPOT
lane, where macOS keeps it off the proxy and resolves it itself.

The fixture offers four shapes on purpose:
  ts.example            + tail9999.ts.example   two overlay zones, one covering
                                                the other (the suffix collapse)
  ovl.corp.example      an overlay zone the user has put in the CORP lane, where
                        their rule has to win even though it cannot resolve there
  mixed.corp.example    a CGNAT nameserver AND a public one — somebody's
                        intranet, which stays the human's call
  102.100.in-addr.arpa  a reverse zone, which is noise
